WEBVTT

00:00:53.550 --> 00:01:00.000
Growing up in the suburban town of Weathersfield, Connecticut, in the late 1950s, the three

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.310
children of Robbins and Meg Barstow, became interested in the history of our country.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:12.350
Young Daniel wanted to learn about the Native Americans who inhabited this land before it

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.850
was taken over by white settlers from Europe.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:21.070
Mary became interested in the American Revolution when the colonists fought for and won their

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.470
independence from Great Britain.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:30.000
David focused his attention on the future and the possibilities which rockets held.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.520
for expanding human exploration into the heavens.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.020
So in 1954, we bought a large camping tent,

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.700
which we used as a means for traveling around the country

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.190
to visit historic sites and natural wonders

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.830
during summer vacations,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.630
combining the fun of camping

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.390
with learning about America's past and present.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.560
We thought friends and neighbors

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.000
might be interested in our families

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.800
family travel adventures, so we took home movies of our trips and put on neighborhood

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.220
movie shows when we returned each year.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:19.630
That was half a century ago, and now in the year 2000, when I'm 81 years old, I have transferred

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:27.420
to video the 16 millimeter movies which I took on all these summer camping trips so

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.000
that we could bring back some of those memories.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.240
our family and interested friends. It was really quite an extraordinary goal that we set for

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:44.690
ourselves. We wanted to see parts of the country that we'd read about in magazines and seen pictures

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:50.030
of and heard about other people going, but we knew there was no way that we could see the United States

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:58.280
unless we found an inexpensive means of travel and staying overnight, which seemed to have,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.000
as an answer, camping.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.070
WELL, WE WERE INVOLVED IN

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.410
THE FIRST COUPLE OF SUMMERS

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:06.770
WHEN WE WERE INVOLVED IN

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.550
WORKING OUT THESE PLANS.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.650
WE TRIED OUT THE TENT TO SEE

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.730
IF THAT WAS THE WAY TO GO.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.870
WE HAD MOVED INTO OUR NEW

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:28.100
HOME IN WEDNESDAY.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.110
WHEN WE WERE INVOLVED IN

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.000
WORKING OUT THESE PLANS,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.000
in 1951, just after Daniel was born.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.890
And for several years, we enjoyed backyard picnicking

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:43.800
and vacationing around home. But in 1954,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.580
we undertook a new approach to summer fun.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.990
Some friends of ours had gone camping in tents

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:56.090
and found this kind of recreation to be both enjoyable and inexpensive.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:00.000
After considerable research, we decided to order a large

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.680
tent ourselves from Sears Roebuck the tent had to be big enough to provide

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.710
sleeping quarters for a family of five it was an exciting family event when it

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:20.230
finally arrived at our front door we decided to try setting up the tent for

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:25.920
the first time in our own backyard and of course everyone wanted to participate

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.130
in the big project

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:41.510
We wanted to see how fast we could get it spread out on the ground and then mounted on

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:45.180
the tall two-sectioned tent poles.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:51.200
The tent had a canvas floor and a mosquito net screen door in the front, as well as screened

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.650
side walls for ventilation.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:00.000
We found that there was room in it for three single sleeping bags and one double bag.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.820
bag to be laid out as well as space to change clothes. So we went on our first tent excursion

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:14.590
to a Connecticut public campground on the shore of Long Island Sound, where we shared

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:27.570
common facilities with other campers at Rocky Neck State Park. We spent several nights in

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.000
the tent there and we enjoyed.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.350
days sunning ourselves on the beautiful sandy beach and being refreshed by the

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:50.920
cool salt ocean water. Daniel was just being introduced to the sea and David had

00:05:50.920 --> 00:06:00.000
learned that it was easier to float in salt water than in fresh. We ate lunch in the nearest shade

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.380
outside our tent. After this fun time in Connecticut, we decided next to venture

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:13.360
further afield and go out of state to a public campground up in New Hampshire,

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.170
which some friends had recommended to us.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:30.000
It was White Lake State Park. We set the tent up there among the trees using

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.800
the trunk of our car for a pantry and storage space. We camped out there for a whole week

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:43.630
and found that families in neighboring campsites were invariably friendly and fun to share

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:48.430
activities with. One night, we had heard a noise and shined our flashlight on a masked

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:55.460
visitor who turned out to have been a raccoon, as evidenced by tiny footprints in the dirt

00:06:55.460 --> 00:07:00.000
outside our tent flap. Before moving to Wethersfield,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.040
Our family had lived in Niantic, Connecticut, where Robbins had taught school.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:08.580
Two of his former teaching colleagues, Ed and Jerry Randler,

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.420
stopped by to visit us at this campsite,

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.960
which was especially fun because Jerry had been Mary's first grade teacher.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:21.580
On our way home from this trip, we enjoyed riding a skeemobile

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:24.660
up to the top of New Hampshire's Mount Cranmore,

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:30.000
from which we had a fantastic view of New England hills and countryside.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.690
It was scarier riding the cars down than up.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:50.520
We also stopped at Benson's Wild Animal Farm in Hudson, New Hampshire to view unfamiliar animals like this haughty camel.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:58.160
And where the children were able to experience the thrill of going for a ride on top of a huge Indian elephant.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:16.830
The following summer, we tried camping out on Cape Cod, Massachusetts, crossing the canal

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:24.470
bridge and driving in our five-seater car with a travel rack on the roof to Ronald C.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:30.000
Nickerson State Forest Park, located on the elbow of the Cape, where we had reserved a

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:40.080
campsite, this time for two weeks. Meg had now made by hand a distinctive colorful

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:46.380
Davy Crockett jacket with white fringes on the arms and pockets for each member

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:53.300
of the family. These special jackets became our standard travel outfits for all our camping

00:08:53.300 --> 00:09:00.000
excursions. We took great pride and joy in them. They served as an identifying family symbol and

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.160
bond wherever we went our individual names were embroidered on the back of

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:21.270
each jacket Danny David Mary Meg and Rob we set up our big tent and other

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:26.400
camping paraphernalia including a Coleman stove for cooking outdoor meals

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:30.000
in a nice wooded spot for an extended stay

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.360
As always neighboring campers were very friendly and helpful often sharing fruit

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:43.920
juice and other refreshments. This was the year that Walt Disney's Davy Crockett

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.500
television program was wildly popular and all the kids wore coonskin caps. One

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.890
story was that when Davy was attacked by a bear his wife was afraid to take sides

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:57.510
and just kept cheering both of them on until Davy being played here by our Dave

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:00.000
finally was able to grin down.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:07.940
Danny, the mayor. We used our campsite at Nickerson State Park as a base for a

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:12.340
number of sightseeing side trips. We drove out to the sand dunes on the outer

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:18.760
edge of the Cape and visited the Truro Lighthouse, near which there was a steep

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:25.240
headland cliff overlooking the beach and the Atlantic Ocean far below.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:39.620
Meg made sure that the children didn't lean too far out over the edge and then we got our

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:46.430
bathing suits out and went down for a fun romp on the broad sandy beach and some cold

00:10:46.430 --> 00:11:00.000
water play in the ocean breakers.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.620
greatly impressed by the landmark Pilgrim Monument towering over Provincetown at the

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:09.900
far end of Cape Cod, President Theodore Roosevelt had laid the cornerstone for this tremendous

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:16.200
granite shaft in 1907, and we all wanted to make our way up the inclined walkway inside

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:24.180
it to get to the top of the 255-foot tower from which the parking lot looked very small

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:30.000
down below. We had a dramatic view of the long wharf extending out

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:37.030
into the harbor, and looking back, we could see bright blue water on both sides of the

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:39.250
narrow hook-like Cape Peninsula.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:49.920
We enjoyed walking along the quaint, narrow streets of the old town, and at the very tip

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:57.060
of the land, we noted a historical tablet marking the place where the pilgrims first

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.000
landed after their perilous voyage across the Atlantic.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.180
on the Mayflower.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:05.510
On a later side trip up the coast

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:07.550
of Massachusetts, we visited the town

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.290
of Plymouth and examined the famous

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:11.690
Plymouth Rock, designating the area

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:14.050
where the Pilgrims finally disembarked

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:15.830
in 1620 and founded

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.370
the first permanent British

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.490
settlement in what is now New England.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:21.790
We were particularly interested

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:23.590
in the reconstructed house and

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:25.910
fort here because our family

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:27.770
is directly descended from a

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:30.000
William Barstow, who emigrated

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.920
from England to Massachusetts

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.180
in the year 1635

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:35.420
and must have lived

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.160
in a similar setting.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:38.380
In Plymouth,

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:39.540
we also admired

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:40.720
the statue of the friendly

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.240
Indian chief Massasoit,

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:43.900
but we were reminded

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:44.500
that throughout

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:45.700
the colonial period,

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:46.980
great harm was done

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:48.060
to Native Americans

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:49.680
whom the whites displaced.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.150
Back at our

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:53.650
Nickerson Park campsite,

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:56.340
in between outside excursions,

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:57.900
members of the family

00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:59.050
enjoyed relaxing

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:00.000
and reading

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:08.380
comic books, lying on our unzipped sleeping bags opened up for a sunshine airing, or leaning

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:17.930
peacefully against a tree trunk.

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:23.810
One of our all-time favorite family books was Seabird, the story of four generations

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:29.050
of a seagoing family written and illustrated with beautiful paintings by Holling Clancy

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:29.960
Holling.

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:33.110
which we had brought with us this summer.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:37.650
Meg had painted pictures of whales and sailors taken from Seabird on the back of a shirt

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:44.740
she had made for me, and this helped inspire us, before leaving Cape Cod, to take a trip

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:55.060
on a large ferry from Hyannisport across the Sound to the island of Nantucket.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:14:00.000
While there, we visited the famous Whaling Museum, which was of special interest by the

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.040
because some of our Barstow forebears in the early 1800s had been whaling ship captains

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:16.700
and boat builders. Finally, on our way back home, we also stopped at the Mystic Seaport

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:22.690
in Connecticut, where we were able to go aboard the authentically restored old whaling ship,

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:30.000
the Charles W. Morgan. On the grounds of the marvelously reconstructed seaport

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.680
village, there is a variety of buildings, including a small white steeple chapel.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:40.780
Robin's late father, who at that time was a retired congregational minister, met us

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:46.250
at the chapel, and later showed us around a sailboat belonging to a friend of his who

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:47.450
also loved the sea.

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:58.200
Well, we found that we really did enjoy this camping as a family, and we thought it would

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:00.000
be a wonderful way to travel.

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:03.590
without having all that expense and still having the fun.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:06.830
So what we did then was to develop this plan.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:08.980
It was a five-year plan.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:11.460
I worked for the Connecticut Education Association then,

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:14.220
and I had a 30-day vacation every summer,

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.560
and we used every single one of those 30 days

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:20.610
each of the five summers that we lined up for this trip.

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:26.120
We doing a lot of studying on books and atlases and so on,

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:30.000
the parade, family, vacation guide,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.750
in books about national parks we found all the places we wanted to go to and we decided that we

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:43.160
would try it in five different summers in this way we would do new england the rest of new england we

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:48.660
would do in the first summer and that would be sort of uh short distances that we could manage

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:56.670
then we thought we'd try driving out to the central the north central states and the five

00:15:56.670 --> 00:16:00.000
Great Lakes and then the third summer we would

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.780
come down the east coast to the United States

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:04.400
and get all the way down to Florida

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:06.340
and come over to Louisiana

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.500
and cover the southeast that way.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.150
And then the fourth summer,

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:13.230
we would go all the way to the great northwest.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:15.510
That was a big, long trip,

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:18.060
nearly 10,000 miles.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:19.000
And then we also,

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.020
the following summer, 1961 or last summer,

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:23.840
we did the great southwest.

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:27.030
So we had this goal in five summers

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:30.000
to go to every single one of the four

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.990
48 states, so that the kids, when they were asked in school or someplace how many states

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:37.950
they'd been into, they could say 48.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.650
At that time, Hawaii and Alaska were not part of the United States.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:49.290
They didn't join until after we'd started in 1959, so we couldn't drive to them anyplace.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:55.390
But now, this is the story of how we went about these five summers of family camping

00:16:55.390 --> 00:17:00.000
through 48 states.

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:05.650
base of course was our home right here in Wethersfield Connecticut and this is

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:18.240
the place where we started all of our trips 1957 was the summer that we

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:24.240
started our five-year plan and we did New England nearby New England we started

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:29.730
here at our home in Connecticut Wethersfield we went up along the Connecticut

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.660
into Vermont and over through New Hampshire and up into Maine and over to Acadia National Park

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:44.840
at Mount Desert Island. Then we came back down along the coast of Maine and through New Hampshire

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:52.400
again and to Massachusetts and then down through Rhode Island and back to Wethersfield and that

00:17:52.400 --> 00:18:00.000
was 1957. We had a real sense of family purpose when we set out on this 1957.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.700
57 trip. We knew that this was the start of an adventure that would take us five years and many

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:11.540
thousands of miles to complete. We now had a new Rambler cross-country station wagon and with our

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:16.470
camping gear loaded onto two car racks on the roof, we headed out to take in all six New England

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:22.010
states. Our first major stop was in Woodstock, Vermont to see the first congregational church

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:28.310
there. This was where Robin's minister father had had his first parish and across the street from

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.000
the church was the parsonage

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:38.110
where on October 24th, 1919, Robbins Wilcott Barstow Jr. had been born. After growing up

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:42.670
in Hartford, Connecticut, Robbins had gone to Dartmouth College in Hanover, New Hampshire,

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:50.250
where he graduated in 1941. Returning to this beautiful campus with Baker Library overlooking

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:57.080
the green, we went up to the small balcony outside the library tower for a bird's eye

00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:00.000
view of the college quadrangle, the white,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.570
sighted Dartmouth Hall and the surrounding hills. Not far from Hanover, we

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:09.370
sighted from a distance one of New Hampshire's most famous natural

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:15.010
landmarks, the Old Man of the Mountain, also known as the Great Stone Face. A

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:20.480
striking 48-foot-tall rock profile silhouetted against the sky on the

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:26.170
upper cliffs of Profile Mountain, a giant nature-made sculpture. The high

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:30.000
point of this summer's trip no doubt came on our four

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.630
day out, when we went on an excursion to the top of the highest peak in the whole northeastern

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:42.420
part of the country, Mount Washington, in the New Hampshire Presidential Range.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:48.080
We had the fun and excitement of riding all the way to the top on the famous Mount Washington

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:55.670
Cog Railway, which was first put into operation way back in 1869. Pushed by an old-fashioned

00:19:55.670 --> 00:20:00.000
coal-burning steam engine, the passenger car proceeds up the mountainside,

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.880
on wheels and tracks, protected with many safety devices,

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:06.470
taking nearly an hour to reach the summit,

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:10.660
which is 6,288 feet high,

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:15.790
and is almost always covered with clouds and mist.

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:19.470
After a long drive through the interior woodlands of Maine,

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.750
America's easternmost state,

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:24.230
we arrived at our summer's primary destination,

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:27.590
Acadia National Park, on Mount Desert Island,

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:29.480
close to the port of Bar Harbor.

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:34.910
We reached the Black Woods campground just in time to secure one of the last available

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:42.380
campsites and here, right in the midst of towering spruce trees, we set up our camp

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:48.440
for a two-week stay, our longest stopover at a single place. This time we had brought

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:55.320
with us some large tarpaulins and clotheslines which we attached to trees and our tent to

00:20:55.320 --> 00:21:00.000
provide an extended area of covered space for when it rained. It was a wonderful

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:13.080
relaxing place to be, whether preparing meals, carving wood, or riding a rocking tree stump.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:18.700
This was the first time we had stayed at a national park, and we were tremendously impressed

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:24.470
with the many tours and educational programs led by uniformed rangers. From the top of Mount Cadillac,

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:28.890
we could look out over the porcupine islands, extending in a row out into the Atlantic from

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:30.000
beautiful Frenchman's.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:36.010
bay. We learned a great deal about natural history and ocean life from the rangers. Looking way down

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:41.090
from the edge of otter cliffs, we could see that the deeper the water, the darker its blue color

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:47.550
was. At Thunder Hole, the waves come crashing in and make a sound like the clap of thunder

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:54.150
splashing spray high into the sunlit air. The children found it endlessly fascinating to examine

00:21:54.150 --> 00:22:00.000
the snails, sea urchins, crabs, and other saltwater sea life in the tide pools among the shoreline rock

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.320
at low tide and our two weeks here went by all too fast. On our long drive home

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:11.120
to Connecticut we stopped at two historic sites in Massachusetts, Lexington

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:15.740
where the first shots were fired on the village green here on April 19th 1775

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:20.780
sparking the American Revolutionary War and nearby Concord Bridge where British

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:25.580
troops were turned back by determined American Patriots known as Minutemen to

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:30.000
honor whom this statue was erected. Finally in Royal

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.940
Island we had a hasty picnic lunch on Narragansett Bay to end our 1957 summer

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:49.910
odyssey. 1958 was the year that we did the north central states in the five

00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:55.950
Great Lakes. We started again at our home here in Hartford went over across

00:22:55.950 --> 00:23:00.000
into New York State up the Hudson River and then all the way up

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:05.560
across New York to Buffalo and Niagara Falls and then through a little part of Canada down

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:06.500
to Detroit.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:12.680
From Detroit we ran on up to Michigan to the top of the state and the Mackinac Bridge and

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:18.120
then over the northern part of Michigan and down through Wisconsin to Madison and then

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:26.450
we took a side trip up to St. Paul and Minneapolis, Minnesota and then on the way back we stopped

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:30.000
in Milwaukee and then we had to head home just as fast as we could.

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:35.320
to get home before the 30 days was up that year.

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:40.740
And we did actually swim in all five of the Great Lakes.

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:45.980
In Lake Ontario, in Lake Erie, Lake Huron, Lake Michigan,

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:49.490
and Lake Superior.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:51.550
This year, we had a new piece of equipment,

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:53.870
a neat little one-wheel trailer to carry some

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:55.740
of our extra camping gear.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:59.020
Heading west, we crossed over into New York State

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.000
and drove up along the highway.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:07.380
Hudson River on the Storm King Highway, stopping en route to see the West Point Military Academy

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:12.610
and across the river, Franklin D. Roosevelt's home at Hyde Park.

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:20.410
After camping overnight at Bear Mountain Park, we enjoyed visiting the Catskill Game Farm.

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:27.240
It was an impressive experience for the children to see live giraffes for the first time.

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:39.140
They also had fun learning how a baby elephant uses its remarkable trunk.

00:24:39.280 --> 00:25:00.000
Driving across New York State, we stopped in Rochester to see the George Eastman House of Photography with one of the largest photographs.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.860
photographic collections in the world.

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:05.080
Then it was on to one of the world's

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:07.100
greatest natural wonders,

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:10.500
the incomparable Niagara Falls.

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:13.320
The Niagara River flows north from Lake Erie

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:17.000
to Lake Ontario and separates New York State on the east

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:20.360
from the Canadian province of Ontario on the west.

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:21.420
Midway along the river,

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:23.740
there is a tremendous double waterfall

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:27.340
with a thunderous drop of over 160 feet.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:30.000
It is estimated that 164,000

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.840
cubic feet of water per second plunge over the combined Falls goat Island

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:39.980
separates the narrower American Falls from the giant horseshoe Falls on the

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:44.180
Canadian side from goat Island you can descend in an elevator to the base of

00:25:44.180 --> 00:25:48.140
the Falls where you are provided with bright yellow raincoats for a guided

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:53.750
tour around the cave of the winds being enveloped in spray and mist while

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:59.800
looking up from the bottom of the Falls makes this a really cool experience

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:11.310
after crossing the rainbow bridge into canada we went downstream a couple of miles for a thrilling

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:16.910
ride in a cable car out over the whirlpool rapids caused by the 40 mile an hour current

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:24.760
running into a sharp turn in the narrow gorge then we returned for a ride down to the river's

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:30.000
edge and a boat trip in the famous made of the mist getting yet another

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:38.300
underneath view of this water wonder. Before leaving our Niagara campground, we

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:43.540
took a closer look at the curved 2,000-foot crest line of the awesome

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:48.760
Horseshoe Falls from the Canadian side, where 90% of the water flows over the

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:53.340
fall. After this, we drove through Canada, across the southern part of the

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:58.070
province of Ontario, to Detroit, Michigan, and the nearby college town of Ann Arbor,

00:26:58.070 --> 00:27:00.000
where we were warmly welcomed by some old

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:03.840
time college friends of Robbins and Meg, Robert and Margaret Blood and their

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:07.360
children, at whose home we stayed for a couple of nights while sightseeing in

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:12.160
Detroit. In the auto capital of the world, we visited the Ford Rotunda

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:18.820
Exhibition Center and were taken for a scary ride on the racetrack used to test

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:26.880
new cars with steeply banked curves. We then headed up to northern Michigan and

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:30.000
crossed one of the great engineering wonders of the modern world.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:32.300
the Mackinac Straits Bridge,

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:34.650
four and one-half miles long,

00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:37.610
linking Michigan's lower and upper peninsulas.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:40.550
It had just been completed the year before.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:43.710
Further north, at Salt-Saint-Marie,

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:47.460
we were amazed at the giant locks of the Sioux Canal

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:49.260
running between Michigan and Canada

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:51.680
and linking Lake Superior in the north

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:54.070
to the lower Great Lakes of Huron and Michigan.

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:57.730
At that time, these locks were the largest and busiest on Earth,

00:27:57.730 --> 00:28:00.000
passing an average of about 95,

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.840
vessels a day, some of them giant freighters, raised and lowered from one water level to

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:07.530
the other in about 30 minutes.

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:11.870
Not far to the west of the Sioux is Hiawatha Country, where we took a short river cruise

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:17.110
to the Taquamenon Falls, one of the sites celebrated in Longfellow's famous poem about

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:19.990
early Native American life and legends.

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:24.820
Our next state was Wisconsin, whose capital city is Madison.

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:30.000
This is where Robbins had lived as a child for six years, when his father was minister

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.000
of the First Congregational Church there from 1924 to 1930.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:39.030
We had the privilege of attending Sunday morning services

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:41.370
at the church, which was still being served

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.910
by the minister who had succeeded Robbins' father,

00:28:43.910 --> 00:28:46.200
Dr. Alfred Swan.

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:49.320
The Swans invited us for dinner at the parsonage

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:53.710
at 21 Bascom Place in Madison, where Robbins had lived

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:58.160
with his parents and two younger brothers until 1930.

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:00.000
A giant statue of the legend

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.840
legendary woodsman Paul Bunyan and his great blue ox babe,

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.580
provided memorable images for us to take back

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:10.030
from our overnight side trip to Minneapolis, Minnesota,

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:13.910
the last of this summer's new states.

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:17.830
Before heading south to Chicago in the long drive back east,

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:20.790
we had one more exciting Wisconsin experience

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:24.070
at the Milwaukee County Baseball Stadium,

00:29:24.070 --> 00:29:28.070
attending our first major league baseball game.

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:30.000
The Milwaukee Braves had won

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.860
the World Series in 1957, the year before. And in the game we saw, the Braves beat the

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:42.590
San Francisco Giants by a score of 10 to 1. The Braves hit three home runs, including

00:29:42.590 --> 00:29:49.570
one by Hank Aaron in the bottom of the seventh. Aaron went on to become the all-time career

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:58.760
home run leader with a total of 755 homers. Now, remember what I said about our having

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:00.000
swum in every one?

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:05.750
of the five great lakes? Well, the water looked pretty much alike in all of them. So we positioned

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:12.190
ourselves in the form of a letter like M for Lake Michigan to identify each one of them when we went

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:29.600
into it. Here's O for Lake Ontario, E for Lake Erie, H for Lake Huron, and finally S for Lake Superior.

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:39.480
We thought this was a pretty good record by which to wrap up our 1958 summer journey to the North Central States.

00:30:39.580 --> 00:30:49.650
This was not really the end of our travels, so even though 1958 was the year of Zorro on Walt Disney's television show,

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:56.730
we asked David not to leave this lettering behind us, or even the Z he had made as the mark of Zorro.

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:15.920
1959 was our year for going south and it was our longest trip yet we added 14

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:22.140
new states to our list of having visited plus the District of Columbia

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:30.000
Washington DC we started out as usual in our own very

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:31.040
Mothersfield, Connecticut,

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:32.670
and on down,

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:35.710
and we spent some time in New Jersey,

00:31:36.790 --> 00:31:38.490
and then we went on down

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:40.350
to Pennsylvania and Philadelphia

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:42.710
and on down through Delaware

00:31:42.710 --> 00:31:44.410
and Maryland

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:46.850
and on to Washington, D.C.

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:48.830
After doing Washington,

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:50.650
we went on down through Virginia

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:53.330
and along the coast here

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:57.200
to North Carolina

00:31:57.200 --> 00:32:00.000
and then down to...

00:32:00.030 --> 00:32:07.020
through South Carolina and Georgia, and then Florida, the whole Florida peninsula down to

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:14.750
the Keys at the bottom, and then back up through the middle of Florida, and down across the

00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:20.760
Panhandle, and through a little bit of Alabama and Mississippi, and to Louisiana, and New

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:24.580
Orleans, and from New Orleans, we're going back up through Mississippi, Alabama again,

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:29.350
up to Tennessee, Chattanooga, Tennessee, and then on up through the Blue Ridge Parkway

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:30.000
and Virginia

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.430
and through a corner of West Virginia and then to Pennsylvania again and then home as fast as we

00:32:36.430 --> 00:32:46.180
could. We spent a lot of time getting ready for this extended 1959 trip. We had to learn a lot

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:52.500
about station wagon living because for 30 days our family of five would be cooped up in our well-packed

00:32:52.500 --> 00:33:00.000
station wagon for as many as five or six hours a day traveling to all the historic and scenic places we had picked out to visit in

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:06.410
the South. This was the year that hula hoops became a fad, and all three of our children

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:11.530
found that swinging their hips around to keep a hula hoop from falling down was an excellent

00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:17.980
way to exercise in between long stretches of driving. In addition to the hoop, we took

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:22.530
with us favorite books about the places we were going to, like Men of the Mississippi

00:33:22.530 --> 00:33:26.090
and Paddle to the Sea by Holling Clancy Holling.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:40.440
On all our trips, we liked to collect window stickers for each state we visited and for

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:45.580
major attractions that we particularly enjoyed. By this time, we were running out of space

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:50.580
to put stickers on the side windows of our car, but each sticker brought back pleasant

00:33:50.580 --> 00:33:54.910
memories as well as enhancing our anticipation of new sights to see.

00:33:56.150 --> 00:34:00.000
Finally, we were ready to depart on a journey.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:10.930
that would take us a total of nearly six thousand miles sightseeing south with an average daily

00:34:10.930 --> 00:34:17.810
driving distance of approximately 200 miles sometimes considerably more sometimes much less

00:34:19.390 --> 00:34:25.200
our mode of traveling was for each person to take with them a small sack of personal

00:34:25.200 --> 00:34:30.000
belongings like books papers games and extra clothes to keep with them in the car

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:35.290
while driving. Meg and I occupied the front seat and the three kids were in the

00:34:35.290 --> 00:34:41.320
back rotating window seats on a daily basis. We had the trunk and roof of the

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:46.880
car fully loaded as well as the little trailer behind and though I did most of

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:54.450
the driving Meg took the wheel a good share of the time. The first day we drove

00:34:54.450 --> 00:35:00.000
all the way to Wachung, New Jersey where we set up our new camping equipment

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:05.380
in the extensive yard of the new country home of Meg's parents.

00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:09.800
We had no problem maneuvering the car to just the spot we wanted, because the wheel on our

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:14.840
little trailer would just reverse itself when we backed up, and then revolve again when

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:25.510
we started forward. Even driving over bumpy ground didn't seem to bother it.

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:30.000
For this longer trip, we had developed a new system to make our many

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.840
single night stopovers easier to manage.

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:34.320
When we arrived at a campsite,

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:37.260
every member of the family had certain assigned tasks.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.600
The trailer served as our traveling pantry.

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:42.420
It was carefully packed with a special place

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:45.800
for every piece of equipment, including a portable stove,

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:48.740
a folding metal table with little folding stools,

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.900
which were Danny's responsibility,

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:55.720
an ice chest, food boxes, and cooking and dining utensils.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:57.560
We had learned that for many people,

00:35:57.560 --> 00:35:59.540
camping is an end in itself.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:04.660
We'd like to set up a tent and stay in one selected place for several weeks or more.

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:08.480
For us, however, camping was a means to an end.

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:14.430
It enabled us to travel far and wide at minimum expense.

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:21.920
We enjoyed camping out, but our primary objective was seeing new places and experiencing America's

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:24.910
history and natural wonders.

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:30.000
It broadened our perspectives to meet people in other parts of our great country and to

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:40.380
what life is like in other places. Using a Coleman stove to cook on and a Coleman lantern

00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:47.680
to see by at night, we had a relatively easy time preparing for a nutritional campsite

00:36:47.680 --> 00:37:00.000
meal together, even after a long day's drive. Our sleeping arrangements this year were

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.850
radically different. We had left behind the large cumbersome canvas tent that all five

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:12.150
of us had slept in and that took so long to set up and dismantle. Instead, we had purchased

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:19.110
a new kind of self-supporting, quickly assembled dome-like tent called a pop tent. After fitting

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:30.000
the ribs together and pressing down on the top to engage a central latch, you just lifted the tent into the

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:40.480
air, and pop! The bottom spread out to the width of its canvas floor. Meg and I had a

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:46.160
double sleeping bag, which we used comfortably in this handy zipper-doored little tent. For

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:50.720
the three children, we now had worked out a unique system which enabled all three of

00:37:50.720 --> 00:37:55.480
them to sleep inside the station wagon. I had built a wooden platform to fit on the

00:37:55.480 --> 00:38:00.000
floor in the rear of the wagon. The platform contained three compartments, specially

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:06.520
sized to hold the small individual suitcases which each of the children had for their clothes

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:13.630
and other necessities. Each child also had an individual sleeping bag and air mattress which

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:20.180
we could unroll and pump up right in the car. In addition, I had built a companion wooden platform

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:24.430
which when the back seat was lowered could be pulled over and dropped down to provide an

00:38:24.430 --> 00:38:30.000
extended floor space upon which to spread Mary and David's sleeping bags. We used a foot

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:35.490
pump to inflate the air mattresses. Daniel was short enough so that when the back of the front

00:38:35.490 --> 00:38:42.100
seat was lowered, he could sleep crosswise there in his sleeping bag. So everyone was snug and

00:38:42.100 --> 00:38:48.800
comfortable, even when it rained. Sometimes for one-day excursions, we would leave the trailer

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:55.040
and tent behind, as when we drove down to Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, where a giant statue

00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:59.670
of the state's Quaker founder, William Penn,

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:06.000
down from the top of the City Hall.

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:13.060
In Philadelphia, we strolled past the famous Liberty Bell to historic Independence Hall,

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:19.580
where on July 4, 1776, representatives of the 13 original American colonies issued a

00:39:19.580 --> 00:39:26.390
public declaration of independence from Britain inaugurating the five-year Revolutionary War,

00:39:26.390 --> 00:39:29.090
which is celebrated every year with fireworks.

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:38.500
Adjacent to Pennsylvania is the small state of Delaware, known as the first state because

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:42.710
it was the first to ratify the U.S. Constitution after the war.

00:39:42.710 --> 00:39:46.690
During the war, the first American victory was achieved when General George Washington

00:39:46.690 --> 00:39:52.690
secretly crossed the Delaware River with his small army on Christmas night in 1776 to surprise

00:39:52.690 --> 00:39:55.850
and capture the Hessian troops in Trenton, New Jersey.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:40:00.000
We visited the crossing site with Meg's parents.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.740
visited the reconstructed wood cabins at Valley Forge Pennsylvania where

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:09.760
Washington's men suffered so much during the bitter winter of 1777 and 78.

00:40:09.760 --> 00:40:15.640
After Pennsylvania and Delaware we moved on to Maryland and stopped at Fort

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:20.200
McHenry where during the later war of 1812 with England Francis Scott Key was

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:24.220
inspired to write the Star Spangled Banner. You can see that it is still

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:30.000
waving today. Then we came to the city of Washington DC and after spending the

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.500
night with some friends outside the city, we took a whole day touring our nation's capital,

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:40.080
located in the District of Columbia, established as the seat of national government in 1790.

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:46.940
We started with the Washington Monument, completed in 1888 and standing 555 feet tall in the middle

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:52.820
of the mall. From its top, one can see, looking east, the Capitol building, where Congress meets

00:40:52.820 --> 00:40:58.650
to enact laws. Its dome was being renovated at the time of our visit, but we did get in to see

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:00.000
both the House and the Senate

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.770
Chambers. Looking north from the monument, one sees the White House, the

00:41:05.770 --> 00:41:08.150
distinguished home of all our presidents,

00:41:08.150 --> 00:41:12.510
and open daily for walk-through visits by ordinary citizens like us.

00:41:12.510 --> 00:41:16.270
In 1959, President Dwight D. Eisenhower

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:19.930
was nearing the end of his presidency, and though, of course, we did not see him

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:20.630
in person,

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:24.140
we were deeply impressed with his residency. Looking

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:28.550
west from the top of the Washington Monument, we see the beautiful

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:30.000
Lincoln Memorial.

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:36.430
bordering the Potomac River. To us, this was the most moving and meaningful of all,

00:41:36.430 --> 00:41:45.610
memorializing the Civil War president who was shot and killed in 1865.

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:50.570
Above his statue are inscribed the words, "In this temple, as in the hearts of the

00:41:50.570 --> 00:41:55.210
people for whom he saved the Union, the memory of Abraham Lincoln is

00:41:55.210 --> 00:42:00.000
enshrined forever." Finally, looking south,

00:42:00.120 --> 00:42:04.920
We find the strikingly architectured Jefferson Memorial on the edge of the

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:09.420
tidal basin, honoring the country's third president, Thomas Jefferson,

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:12.420
who had sworn upon the altar of God,

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:17.120
eternal hostility against any form of tyranny over the mind of man.

00:42:18.010 --> 00:42:21.410
From Washington, we crossed the Potomac to the state of Virginia,

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:25.520
where we paid a visit to Jefferson's marvelous mountainside home at Monticello.

00:42:25.820 --> 00:42:30.000
We were fascinated by its unique structural layout and by his intriguing house

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:34.960
inventions. His self-designed tombstone identifies him as the author of the

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:38.820
Declaration of Independence, the Virginia Statute for Religious Freedom, and

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:44.270
father of the University of Virginia. We also visited Mount Vernon, the elegant

00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:47.930
Virginia home of General George Washington, who became the country's

00:42:47.930 --> 00:42:53.850
first elected president. Then it was on to Williamsburg, a fine restoration of the

00:42:53.850 --> 00:42:58.660
colonial capital of Virginia. It was here that the seeds were planted for the

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:00.000
American War of Independence.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:07.640
when Patrick Henry cried out, "Give me liberty or give me death." Historically we

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:12.060
need to remember, however, that he was then speaking of freedom only for the

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:18.390
white colonists and not for the thousands of black slaves whom they owned at

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:30.000
that time. Not far from Williamsburg is Yorktown where the

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:32.780
the final battle of the Revolutionary War was fought.

00:43:32.780 --> 00:43:43.220
Back in our hometown of Wethersfield in Connecticut, we had previously visited the historic Webb

00:43:43.220 --> 00:43:51.620
House, where Washington and the French General Comte de Rochambeau had planned the campaign

00:43:51.620 --> 00:43:58.610
ending at Yorktown in 1781, which at last brought victory and independence from Britain

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:00.000
for the Americans.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:07.820
When visiting the site of this concluding Revolutionary War battle in Yorktown,

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:12.670
we imagined what it must have been like first to see the British flag flying and

00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:22.640
then to see taking its place the flag of the fledgling United States of America.

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:28.820
The nearby Moor House was where British General Cornwallis surrendered to Washington.

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:38.240
continuing down the east coast to north carolina we admired the monument at kitty hawk which marks

00:44:38.240 --> 00:44:45.350
the site of the wright brothers first powered airplane flight in 1903 in south carolina we

00:44:45.350 --> 00:44:49.110
stopped at charleston at the site where the first shot of the fateful war between

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:54.310
the states was fired when south carolina seceded from the union in 1860 and shelled

00:44:54.310 --> 00:45:00.000
the federal garrison on fort sumther in the harbor we rushed through georgia after

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:07.110
to seeing a water spout in the distance, which we were afraid might turn into a tornado.

00:45:07.110 --> 00:45:11.650
And then we were in Florida, where David in particular was eager to visit Cape Canaveral,

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:14.450
the site of early rocket launchings.

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:21.410
It was on July 29, 1959, that we visited Cape Canaveral, later to become Cape Kennedy.

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:26.200
We could scarcely have imagined at that time that almost exactly 10 years later, on July

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:30.000
20, 1969, two human beings from America.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:35.800
America would actually set foot upon the moon and then return safely to Earth from outer space.

00:45:36.920 --> 00:45:42.060
Because there was so much to see and do in the Miami area at the southern end of Florida,

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:46.960
when we got there, we found a trailer camp which we could use as a base for several days,

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:51.360
leaving our tent and little trailer there while we went on one-day excursions in the car.

00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:56.150
We saw what the fabled Miami Beach Resort area looked like

00:45:56.150 --> 00:46:00.000
and found a public part of the beach where we could go swimming ourselves.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:09.780
in the Atlantic Ocean, which still had breakers, but those waters were somewhat warmer in this southerly clime.

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:26.440
We drove out to one of the Florida Keys at the bottom tip of the state, Key Largo, a low, sand-covered island with coral reefs in the water and interesting shells on the sand and rocks next to the lighthouse.

00:46:30.440 --> 00:46:34.060
Then we made an all-day trip to the Everglades National Park

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:38.180
looking for exotic birds and occasional alligators

00:46:38.180 --> 00:46:43.050
as we made our way along the wooden walkway through the marsh.

00:46:51.590 --> 00:46:55.290
Outside the park, there was an alligator farm

00:46:55.290 --> 00:46:57.670
where a lot of them were penned up.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.900
And a trainer showed us how he could grab an alligator's mouth without getting bitten and without, apparently, harming the gator.

00:47:07.900 --> 00:47:29.030
Animal slides seem to offer a popular diversion, not just for this small alligator, but also for this colorful parrot at another exhibition attraction called Parrot Jungle.

00:47:32.620 --> 00:47:38.110
It was fun here to have all three of the children provide perches for these five

00:47:38.110 --> 00:47:53.830
parrots all at the same time. These pink flamingos were a pretty sight. But the most exciting and

00:47:53.830 --> 00:48:00.000
dramatic place we visited in Miami was the Miami's Aquarium, where for the very first time we

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:07.760
saw live, performing dolphins. None of us had ever seen so beautiful and graceful a

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:16.240
creature. We were absolutely enthralled at its skill and obvious intelligence, whether

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:28.360
leaping through a hoop, ringing a school bell, or playing basketball. The dolphin seemed

00:48:28.360 --> 00:48:30.000
to like the cheers and applause.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:37.040
As we gave it when it succeeded, we learned that these were bottlenose dolphins captured

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:40.630
in ocean waters off the coast of Florida.

00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:44.200
There, this time we made it.

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:49.380
Viewing the dolphins through an underwater window with a diver and some giant sea turtles,

00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:55.530
we could see how they used their flat horizontal tails to propel themselves through the water.

00:48:55.530 --> 00:49:00.000
Indeed, their tail strokes were powerful enough to send them leaping out of the water

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:05.550
up into the air several times their own length. Dolphins like these have

00:49:05.550 --> 00:49:09.690
frequently been observed in the wild leaping high above the ocean surface and

00:49:09.690 --> 00:49:14.740
their trainers here had encouraged them to extend that natural behavior by

00:49:14.740 --> 00:49:20.640
rewarding them with fish and other positive reinforcements. Their incredible

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:30.000
coordination demonstrated a high level of intercommunication. Now here is Smiley at one

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:35.560
time the highest leaping dolphin in the world, thrusting itself 21 feet up into the air.

00:49:35.560 --> 00:49:41.920
Then I had the unforgettable experience of actually playing deck tennis with a dolphin.

00:49:41.920 --> 00:49:46.080
I threw this little red rubber ring into the water, and the dolphin used its beak to throw

00:49:46.080 --> 00:49:50.880
it back to me. I threw the ring in again, and the dolphin again returned it. It was an interspecies

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:58.900
game. After Miami, we drove up through Central Florida to Cypress Gardens.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:06.400
where the southern bells in their colorful gowns were as beautiful as the many varieties of flowers.

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:12.560
Cypress Gardens is famous for its water ski show.

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:16.490
We had never seen anything like this, and it was quite a sight.

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:30.000
These skiers start out in tandem and end up in a human pyramid carrying the flag of the southern Confederacy

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.110
in what they called a salute to Dixie.

00:50:37.110 --> 00:50:43.820
This is a miracle act, a man skiing only on his bare feet.

00:50:43.820 --> 00:50:47.230
Watching the high jump was a thrill.

00:50:47.230 --> 00:51:00.000
And slow motion makes this three-man jump even more thrilling.

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:20.000
the man in the middle. He almost doesn't make it, but does end up okay. The final act was

00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:30.000
the dramatic kite flying ride. Up, up, and away. The next state we stopped in as we headed west

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.800
along the Gulf Coast was Mississippi

00:51:32.800 --> 00:51:35.460
where we enjoyed another saltwater swim

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:38.220
with breakers, this time in the Gulf of Mexico

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:40.080
near the lighthouse at Biloxi.

00:51:40.880 --> 00:51:44.540
We reached the western end of our southern journey

00:51:44.540 --> 00:51:47.270
when we arrived in New Orleans

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:48.990
in the state of Louisiana

00:51:48.990 --> 00:51:51.990
where the Mississippi River reaches the sea.

00:51:53.580 --> 00:51:55.140
In this old world city,

00:51:55.640 --> 00:51:58.610
we were impressed by the exceptional iron grill work

00:51:58.610 --> 00:52:00.000
on balconies and fences.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:08.350
forces, which we learned were largely the work of blacksmith slaves.

00:52:08.350 --> 00:52:12.990
This is the War of 1812 battlefield, where the Americans fired their guns and the British

00:52:12.990 --> 00:52:18.070
kept a coming till they were almost totally wiped out by the small army led by Andrew

00:52:18.070 --> 00:52:24.150
Jackson here, who later became president.

00:52:24.150 --> 00:52:30.000
Heading homeward finally through the state of Alabama, we were upset to see signs of segregation,

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:34.820
restrooms, drinking fountains and lunch counters for whites and blacks. That direct experience

00:52:34.820 --> 00:52:39.600
led our family to support the civil rights movement to end racial discrimination. And

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:45.960
four years later in 1963, the March on Washington led by Martin Luther King Jr. Our first stop

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:50.760
when we reached the state of Tennessee was spectacular Lookout Mountain overlooking the

00:52:50.760 --> 00:52:56.290
Tennessee River and the city of Chattanooga. During the Civil War, this was the site of

00:52:56.290 --> 00:53:00.000
the famous Battle Above the Clouds, a decisive engagement in which

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:05.160
Union forces stormed to the top of the mountain and dislodged the Confederate forces, causing

00:53:05.160 --> 00:53:09.500
them to retreat back through Georgia to Atlanta.

00:53:09.500 --> 00:53:14.380
A visit to the great Chickamauga Dam helped us to appreciate the far-reaching significance

00:53:14.380 --> 00:53:19.340
of the Tennessee Valley Authority, which President Franklin Roosevelt had set up during the Depression

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:26.040
years of the 1930s to bring rural electrification and flood control to this whole area.

00:53:26.040 --> 00:53:30.000
Then a visit to the American Museum of Atomic Energy at Oak Ridge, Tennessee, and like

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:33.840
about the beginnings of the dangerous new atomic age we had entered at the

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:39.520
close of World War II in the 1940s. Crossing over into western North Carolina,

00:53:39.520 --> 00:53:43.820
we visited a reconstructed pioneer village which was the setting for a

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:48.860
tragic play called "Unto These Hills," dramatizing the forced expulsion in the

00:53:48.860 --> 00:53:54.060
1830s of 20,000 Cherokee Indians from their mountain homes along a trail of

00:53:54.060 --> 00:54:00.000
tears a thousand miles to Oklahoma. We stayed for an extra day at a camp

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:04.400
in the Great Smoky Mountains National Park in North Carolina.

00:54:04.400 --> 00:54:08.500
We thought that the rarefied atmosphere of this mountainside site

00:54:08.500 --> 00:54:12.080
would be a good place to practice our outdoor cooking skills.

00:54:12.080 --> 00:54:16.280
So each of us took turns at flipping pancakes with mixed results.

00:54:16.280 --> 00:54:19.020
This is David, and here's Mary.

00:54:19.020 --> 00:54:22.950
Uh-oh.

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:30.000
It appeared that Danny was able to flip his invisible

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:38.900
pancake so high up into the sky that when it came down, he couldn't avoid having it

00:54:38.900 --> 00:54:46.110
land on his face. As we traveled north on the beautiful Blue Ridge Parkway, we enjoyed

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:55.110
stopping at Blowing Lock, where unusual air currents keep light objects blowing upward.

00:54:55.110 --> 00:55:00.000
We followed the parkway into western Virginia and drove through the Shenandoah National

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:05.400
Park on the Skyline Drive these magnificent roadways extending along the

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:09.660
crests of mountains for hundreds of miles are the longest highways in the

00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:14.940
world designed purely for recreational motoring we had tremendous views

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:20.230
looking down into deep valleys on both sides of the highway our one stop in

00:55:20.230 --> 00:55:25.560
West Virginia was at Harper's Ferry where the abolitionist John Brown had

00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:30.000
tried to free some slaves just before the Civil War then back in Pennsylvania

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:34.720
Pennsylvania, we stopped to visit Gettysburg, the site of the Civil War's most decisive

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:38.740
battle and the turning point of that great struggle. In one of the most terrible and

00:55:38.740 --> 00:55:44.700
tragic battles ever fought in July 1863, the southern forces of General Robert E. Lee,

00:55:44.700 --> 00:55:50.000
in a desperate charge across this bloody field, were finally turned back by the Union forces

00:55:50.000 --> 00:55:55.930
with a horrendous loss of life on both sides. In all, more than 50,000 men were counted

00:55:55.930 --> 00:56:00.000
lost in this one three-day battle. It was here on November 19th

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:05.680
1863, the President Abraham Lincoln delivered his famous Gettysburg Address, praying that

00:56:05.680 --> 00:56:10.940
from these honored dead, the living may take increased devotion to the cause of one nation

00:56:10.940 --> 00:56:15.600
under God, dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal.

00:56:16.930 --> 00:56:22.590
This was a sad but significant piece of America's history with which to conclude our 1959 journey,

00:56:22.750 --> 00:56:30.000
and that's the end of part one of these Marstow family camping trip videos.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:39.210
Part 2 will take us up to the Great Northwest and the Great Southwest and that will be in

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:41.780
the next take.

00:56:41.780 --> 00:56:42.780
Thank you very much for watching.

00:56:42.780 --> 00:56:47.270
We hope you have enjoyed reliving these experiences with us.

00:56:47.270 --> 00:56:48.830
Take care and give thanks.
